WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:02.130
SPEAKER: This week, we're
talking about functions,

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.017
service meshes, and more.

00:00:04.017 --> 00:00:05.850
Welcome to this week
in Cloud where we serve

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.800
you the lowest latency news.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.620
First up, flexible
fun with functions.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.870
Google Cloud Functions
is focused on simplicity

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:14.467
and embracing serverless.

00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:16.050
A big part of that
simplicity is being

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:17.700
able to choose your
favorite languages,

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.760
so we're excited to announce
some additional support.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.260
Now anyone can use Python
3.7, Go 1.11 and Node 8.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.600
We're also working on
Node 10, which is in beta,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.290
and have private alphas that
you can sign up for for Go 1.12

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.610
and Java 8.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.640
Dive right in and get
your code in the Cloud.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.320
No matter the
language, your services

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.420
probably handle
traffic, and you don't

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.330
want to bake networking policies
directly into your app code.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.580
That's why everyone is talking
about utilizing service

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.340
meshes and Google Cloud's
recently announced Traffic

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.270
Director.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.860
It's a managed
traffic control plane

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.020
that helps support
separating development

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.510
from operations through
service proxies such as Envoy.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.240
Read the deep dive
and see if this helps

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.228
simplify your application.

00:00:59.228 --> 00:01:01.020
Want to know the best
way to keep a secret?

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.019
With encryption and with Google
Cloud Storage, of course.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:06.390
Seth Fargo, a Google
Cloud developer advocate,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:09.660
has created an open source tool
and library called B3rg1a$ that

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.832
helps simplify
secret management.

00:01:11.832 --> 00:01:13.290
It's also built to
easily integrate

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.560
with Cloud Run, Cloud Functions,
Cloud Build, Kubernetes,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.582
or you can roll
your own wrapper.

00:01:18.582 --> 00:01:20.040
Check out the source
code on GitHub

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.033
and get started with
some great examples.

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:23.700
Now, if you're still
learning about some

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.510
of those great
Google Cloud options,

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.582
we've got you covered
with hands-on training.

00:01:27.582 --> 00:01:29.040
We've got a few
new labs and quests

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.720
to check out from
getting started

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.240
with Cloud Run to a full
guide on modernizing

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.808
with Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:34.808 --> 00:01:37.350
There's even a quest on getting
set up with Google Assistant.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.330
So make sure to check
out the great content

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.117
and try it out for
yourself on Qwiklabs.

00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:43.200
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.300
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.130
Of course, this
week is Google I/O,

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.290
so stay tuned for some
great announcements.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.550
Have any thought or ideas?

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.450
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.830
We'll see you in the Cloud.

